// Three roles, one review, one bounded revision: plan, build, check, finalize.
//
// Nothing here is routed, awaited or terminated by a model. The wiring is
// acyclic, so the whole run is one logical tag of exactly four invocations.
// A model decides only what its role is for: the plan, the work, the review.
// The builder writes the final answer itself, so no one relays its work.
team Planner[planner : Claude]
{
    input statement : string
    input cwd : string
    output plan : string

    task planner(statement, cwd) -> plan
    "
        The task statement is in the file $(statement). Read it in full. The work
        will be done in place on this machine, with $(cwd) as the working
        directory unless the task says otherwise, and not in any OMAR
        workspace. Look at what is there; change nothing.
        Set `plan` to a short numbered plan for a colleague to carry out: what
        to do, in what order, and how to verify that the task's own
        requirements are met.
    "
}

team Builder[builder : Claude]
{
    input statement : string
    input cwd : string
    input plan : string
    input review : string
    output work : string
    output answer : string

    task builder(statement, cwd, plan) -> work
    "
        The task statement is in the file $(statement). Read it in full; it is the
        authority, and this plan is advice:

        $(plan)

        Do the task. It is graded where it lives on this machine, not in
        your OMAR workspace. Make its changes in place, at the paths it names,
        and use $(cwd) as the working directory for anything it leaves
        relative. Your workspace is for your own notes; nothing in it is
        graded.
        When done, set `work` to your response to the task as it stands: the
        text it asks you to deliver, or what you changed on disk, and how you
        verified it.
    "

    task builder(review) -> answer
    "
        A reviewer checked your work on that task:

        $(review)

        Fix each problem it raises, in place as before, or decide it is not
        one. This is the only revision round.
        Then set `answer` to your final response to the task: the text it asks
        you to deliver. If it asks only for changes on disk, one paragraph on
        the final state.
    "
}

team Checker[checker : Claude]
{
    input statement : string
    input cwd : string
    input work : string
    output review : string

    task checker(statement, cwd, work) -> review
    "
        The task statement is in the file $(statement). Read it in full. A
        colleague did the work in place on this machine, with $(cwd) as the
        working directory unless the task says otherwise, and reports this
        result:

        $(work)

        Check it against the task statement by inspecting and running things
        yourself, there and not in your OMAR workspace. Do not take the report
        on trust, and change nothing.
        Set `review` to the single word ACCEPTED if every requirement is met.
        Otherwise set it to a numbered list of concrete problems.
    "
}

team PlanBuildCheck
{
    input statement : string
    input cwd : string
    output answer : string

    p = Planner();
    b = Builder();
    c = Checker();

    statement -> p.statement
    statement -> b.statement
    statement -> c.statement
    cwd -> p.cwd
    cwd -> b.cwd
    cwd -> c.cwd

    p.plan -> b.plan
    b.work -> c.work
    c.review -> b.review
    b.answer -> answer
}

main Bench {
    t = PlanBuildCheck()
}
